/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  chemistry/Hydrazine-formation.dat -- the first 2 dissociations of hydrazine, written from the master:
      N2H6  <=>  Hydrazine + 2 H+

  logK25 and dH are the PRINTED values of the sources below (standard state ideal solution at 1 mol/kg, 298.15 K):
      pK1 = -0.99, dH1 = 38.1 kJ/mol
      pK2 = 8.02, dH2 = 41.7 kJ/mol
  Source: Goldberg, Kishore & Lennen, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 31 (2002) 231; standard state: hypothetical ideal solution of unit molality, 298.15 K, 0.1 MPa (Section 8, p. 359).
  This record carries the sum of the first 2 steps (the sign flips with the direction written).
  The constant-dCp term of the printed dCp is NOT carried: the record grammar has no place for it (a
  van't Hoff integral with a constant dCp is exactly the `analytic` A1, A3, A4 terms; proposed, not done).
  The neutral base is `Hydrazine`, not `N2H4`: the catalogue holds a component N2H4, a derived neutral species of that name would have to carry the `aq` suffix, and check_species_identity pins that suffix as a closed historical class (so `N2H4aq` is refused).  The records are written from the diprotonated master N2H6.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType aqueousSpeciation;
schemaVersion 1;

species Hydrazine; masters ( { ion N2H6; nu 1; } { ion H; nu -2; } );
      logK25 -7.03; dH 79800; source "Goldberg, Kishore & Lennen, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 31 (2002) 231 -- Section 8 summary, p. 360, and Section 7.40, p. 300: pK1 -0.99, dH1 38.1 kJ/mol, pK2 8.02, dH2 41.7 kJ/mol (pK, dH_ion at 298.15 K)";
